IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mistake-domain`
IMPORT `sg-unilateral-mistake`
IMPORT `ew-unilateral-mistake`
IMPORT `au-unilateral-mistake`
IMPORT `us-unilateral-mistake`
IMPORT `mistake-comparison`

§ `The battery — eight real cases and two labelled counterfactuals, run through four jurisdictions`

-- WHAT THIS FILE IS. Every fixture below is the facts of a decided case, as
-- found by the court that decided it, characterised into the shared
-- vocabulary. Every assertion below is what one jurisdiction module says
-- about those facts. Nothing here is a rule of law.
--
-- THE TWO KINDS OF ASSERTION, AND WHY THEY DIFFER IN WEIGHT.
--
--   * Where the fixture is a case decided IN that jurisdiction, the assertion
--     is checkable against the decision, and the comment says which paragraph
--     to check it against. Those are the real tests.
--
--   * Where the fixture is a case decided ELSEWHERE, the assertion records
--     what this model computes, not what any court held. No court has applied
--     Restatement s 153 to Digilandmall. Read those rows as saying only this
--     is what the encoded rule does with these facts, which is exactly what a
--     comparison needs and is not a claim about foreign law. (The phrase is
--     unquoted on purpose: report/verify-quotations.py checks every quoted
--     passage in these modules against the judgment it came from, and a pair
--     of quotation marks around this corpus's own words would be an assertion
--     it could not verify -- which is the failure the check exists to find.)
--
-- HOW THE FACTS WERE CHARACTERISED. Turning a judgment into nine fields is a
-- judgement, and the contestable calls are recorded as forks F-C1 to F-C4 in
-- registers/fork-register.json rather than buried here. Two fixtures are
-- explicitly COUNTERFACTUAL -- one field moved, everything else held -- and
-- are named so that no reader can mistake them for holdings.

§§ `1 -- Chwee Kin Keong v Digilandmall.com, the third appellant`

-- Hewlett Packard laser printers listed at S$66 instead of S$3,854. The third
-- appellant searched the Internet, found the model selling at US$2,000, and
-- ordered 760 of them. Chwee Kin Keong v Digilandmall.com Pte Ltd [2005] SGCA
-- 2 at [93]: "Clearly suspecting a mistake, he pounced on the opportunity to
-- make more money before the mistake was discovered. Thus, we do not think
-- that the judge was wrong to have inferred actual knowledge. The apparent
-- contracts are therefore void."
--
-- FORK F-C1: `a conscious omission to disabuse` rather than `deliberate steps
-- to prevent discovery`. He said nothing and moved fast; the court described
-- haste, not concealment, and at [98] warned that "haste per se in concluding
-- a transaction will not ipso facto suggest actual or constructive
-- knowledge". The stronger characterisation would make Australia relieve, and
-- would erase the divergence this fixture exists to show, so it is recorded
-- and not taken.

`Digilandmall -- the third appellant` MEANS `Mistake case` WITH
    `the case` IS "Chwee Kin Keong v Digilandmall.com Pte Ltd [2005] SGCA 2, the third appellant"
    `what the mistaken party was mistaken about` IS `a term of the contract`
    `the mistake was fundamental` IS TRUE
    `what the other party knew` IS `actual knowledge of the mistake`
    `what the other party did` IS `a conscious omission to disabuse`
    `the mistake had a material adverse effect on the exchange` IS TRUE
    `the mistaken party bears the risk of the mistake` IS FALSE
    `the other party's fault caused the mistake` IS FALSE
    `enforcement would be unconscionable` IS TRUE

-- Singapore: HELD. "The apparent contracts are therefore void" (at [93]).
#ASSERT `what Singapore law does` `Digilandmall -- the third appellant` EQUALS `the contract is void`
-- England: the same common law rule, on Hartog; computed, not held.
#ASSERT `what English law does` `Digilandmall -- the third appellant` EQUALS `the contract is void`
-- Australia: computed. No deliberate steps, so Taylor v Johnson is not engaged
-- and -- on the reading Chwee Kin Keong at [73] attributes to it -- there is
-- no common law void limb to fall back on. This is fork F-AU1 biting.
#ASSERT `what Taylor v Johnson does` `Digilandmall -- the third appellant` EQUALS `the contract stands`
-- United States: computed. Reason to know suffices on its own under s 153(b).
#ASSERT `what the Restatement does` `Digilandmall -- the third appellant` EQUALS `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`

§§ `2 -- Chwee Kin Keong v Digilandmall.com, the second appellant`

-- THE CASE THE WHOLE DOCTRINE EXISTS FOR. At [92]: "even if the court should
-- not have found the second appellant to have actual knowledge of the
-- mistake, it is clear that he had constructive knowledge. While he had
-- thought that there could be a mistake, he went on to snap up a total of 180
-- printers. The court should be entitled to set side the various purchases on
-- the ground of sharp practice or unconscionable conduct."
--
-- Constructive knowledge, plus impropriety, and nothing more. Singapore
-- relieves. Australia's stated test does not reach it. England cannot say.

`Digilandmall -- the second appellant` MEANS `Mistake case` WITH
    `the case` IS "Chwee Kin Keong v Digilandmall.com Pte Ltd [2005] SGCA 2, the second appellant, on the alternative constructive-knowledge basis at [92]"
    `what the mistaken party was mistaken about` IS `a term of the contract`
    `the mistake was fundamental` IS TRUE
    `what the other party knew` IS `reason to suspect a mistake`
    `what the other party did` IS `a conscious omission to disabuse`
    `the mistake had a material adverse effect on the exchange` IS TRUE
    `the mistaken party bears the risk of the mistake` IS FALSE
    `the other party's fault caused the mistake` IS FALSE
    `enforcement would be unconscionable` IS TRUE

-- Singapore: HELD, in the alternative, at [92].
#ASSERT `what Singapore law does` `Digilandmall -- the second appellant` EQUALS `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`
#ASSERT `the contract is voidable in equity` `Digilandmall -- the second appellant`
#ASSERT NOT (`the contract is void at common law` `Digilandmall -- the second appellant`)
-- England: this is the class Great Peace Shipping left unsettled, exactly.
#ASSERT `the case falls in the class Great Peace Shipping left unsettled` `Digilandmall -- the second appellant`
#ASSERT REFUSED `what English law does` `Digilandmall -- the second appellant`
-- Australia: computed. Conscious omission is not deliberate inhibition of discovery.
#ASSERT `what Taylor v Johnson does` `Digilandmall -- the second appellant` EQUALS `the contract stands`
-- United States: computed. s 153(b) needs no impropriety at all.
#ASSERT `what the Restatement does` `Digilandmall -- the second appellant` EQUALS `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`

§§ `3 -- Hartog v Colin & Shields`

-- Chwee Kin Keong at [35]: "the defendants offered to sell to the plaintiff
-- Argentine hare skins, but by mistake offered them at a price per pound
-- instead of at a price per piece. The offer was accepted. However, the court
-- held that the apparent contract was void because the plaintiff must have
-- known and did, in fact, know that there was a mistake in the offer".

`Hartog v Colin and Shields` MEANS `Mistake case` WITH
    `the case` IS "Hartog v Colin & Shields [1939] 3 All ER 566"
    `what the mistaken party was mistaken about` IS `a term of the contract`
    `the mistake was fundamental` IS TRUE
    `what the other party knew` IS `actual knowledge of the mistake`
    `what the other party did` IS `a conscious omission to disabuse`
    `the mistake had a material adverse effect on the exchange` IS TRUE
    `the mistaken party bears the risk of the mistake` IS FALSE
    `the other party's fault caused the mistake` IS FALSE
    `enforcement would be unconscionable` IS TRUE

-- England: HELD. The contract was void.
#ASSERT `what English law does` `Hartog v Colin and Shields` EQUALS `the contract is void`
-- Singapore: computed, and Chwee Kin Keong at [35] treats Hartog as stating
-- Singapore's own common law rule, so this row is close to held.
#ASSERT `what Singapore law does` `Hartog v Colin and Shields` EQUALS `the contract is void`
#ASSERT `what Taylor v Johnson does` `Hartog v Colin and Shields` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what the Restatement does` `Hartog v Colin and Shields` EQUALS `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`

§§ `4 -- Riverlate Properties Ltd v Paul`

-- Chwee Kin Keong at [70]: "the plaintiff lessor, through its agent, had
-- mistakenly omitted to insert a clause to place the defendant lessee under
-- an obligation to bear a part of the cost of the exterior and structural
-- repairs of the demised premises. ... [t]he English Court of Appeal held that
-- as the error was purely that of the lessor and/or its solicitors and the
-- lessee and/or her solicitors had no knowledge of such error and were not
-- guilty of anything approaching sharp practice, there were no grounds to
-- intervene."
--
-- The control case: no knowledge, no impropriety, and every jurisdiction here
-- leaves the contract alone. FORK F-C2 records the risk characterisation --
-- the mistake was the lessor's own solicitors' -- which is what keeps the
-- United States column from diverging.

`Riverlate Properties v Paul` MEANS `Mistake case` WITH
    `the case` IS "Riverlate Properties Ltd v Paul [1975] Ch 133"
    `what the mistaken party was mistaken about` IS `a term of the contract`
    `the mistake was fundamental` IS TRUE
    `what the other party knew` IS `neither knowledge nor reason to suspect`
    `what the other party did` IS `nothing beyond accepting the bargain`
    `the mistake had a material adverse effect on the exchange` IS TRUE
    `the mistaken party bears the risk of the mistake` IS TRUE
    `the other party's fault caused the mistake` IS FALSE
    `enforcement would be unconscionable` IS FALSE

-- England: HELD. No grounds to intervene.
#ASSERT `what English law does` `Riverlate Properties v Paul` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `the other party's conscience was clear` `Riverlate Properties v Paul`
#ASSERT `what Singapore law does` `Riverlate Properties v Paul` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what Taylor v Johnson does` `Riverlate Properties v Paul` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what the Restatement does` `Riverlate Properties v Paul` EQUALS `the contract stands`

§§ `5 -- Taylor v Johnson`

-- Chwee Kin Keong at [72]: two adjoining five-acre parcels sold for "a total
-- price of $15,000"; the vendor "believed it provided for a price of $15,000
-- per acre"; the High Court of Australia affirmed rescission "on the ground
-- that when the purchaser executed the contract he believed the vendor was
-- under some serious mistake or misapprehension about either the price or the
-- value of the land and the purchaser had deliberately set out to ensure that
-- the vendor was not disabused of such a mistake or misapprehension."
--
-- The one fixture where Singapore and Australia agree in full, and Chwee Kin
-- Keong at [73] says as much: "The circumstances of the case amply warranted
-- the intervention of equity."

`Taylor v Johnson` MEANS `Mistake case` WITH
    `the case` IS "Taylor v Johnson (1983) 151 CLR 422"
    `what the mistaken party was mistaken about` IS `a term of the contract`
    `the mistake was fundamental` IS TRUE
    `what the other party knew` IS `reason to suspect a mistake`
    `what the other party did` IS `deliberate steps to prevent discovery`
    `the mistake had a material adverse effect on the exchange` IS TRUE
    `the mistaken party bears the risk of the mistake` IS FALSE
    `the other party's fault caused the mistake` IS FALSE
    `enforcement would be unconscionable` IS TRUE

-- Australia: HELD. Voidable, and set aside.
#ASSERT `what Taylor v Johnson does` `Taylor v Johnson` EQUALS `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`
-- Singapore: computed, and endorsed in terms at [73].
#ASSERT `what Singapore law does` `Taylor v Johnson` EQUALS `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`
#ASSERT REFUSED `what English law does` `Taylor v Johnson`
#ASSERT `what the Restatement does` `Taylor v Johnson` EQUALS `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`

§§ `6 -- Smith v Hughes`

-- The buyer wanted old oats and took new ones, the seller knowing what he
-- believed. The mistake was as to the quality of the thing sold and not as to
-- any term of the sale, and the contract bound. Chwee Kin Keong at [34]
-- expressly left open "whether a mistake as to quality, or the substance of
-- the thing contracted for, is of sufficient gravity to negate an agreement";
-- this model takes the settled answer that it is not, which is fork F3.
--
-- FORK F-US2: the risk characterisation. A buyer who inspects a sample and
-- forms his own view of the oats "is aware, at the time the contract is made,
-- that he has only limited knowledge with respect to the facts to which the
-- mistake relates but treats his limited knowledge as sufficient" -- s 154(b)
-- -- and so bears the risk. That is this model's reading and not a holding.
-- Fixture 6a moves that one field and nothing else.

`Smith v Hughes` MEANS `Mistake case` WITH
    `the case` IS "Smith v Hughes (1871) LR 6 QB 597"
    `what the mistaken party was mistaken about` IS `a basic assumption that is not a term`
    `the mistake was fundamental` IS FALSE
    `what the other party knew` IS `actual knowledge of the mistake`
    `what the other party did` IS `a conscious omission to disabuse`
    `the mistake had a material adverse effect on the exchange` IS TRUE
    `the mistaken party bears the risk of the mistake` IS TRUE
    `the other party's fault caused the mistake` IS FALSE
    `enforcement would be unconscionable` IS FALSE

-- England: HELD, on the correct direction to the jury. The contract bound.
#ASSERT `what English law does` `Smith v Hughes` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what Singapore law does` `Smith v Hughes` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what Taylor v Johnson does` `Smith v Hughes` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what the Restatement does` `Smith v Hughes` EQUALS `the contract stands`
-- All four agree, and NOT for the same reason. Three of them stop because the
-- mistake was not as to a term. The United States stops on risk allocation,
-- having cleared the subject-matter threshold that stopped the other three.
#ASSERT NOT (`the mistake is of a kind the doctrine reaches` `Smith v Hughes`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the threshold conditions of section 153 are met` `Smith v Hughes`)

§§ `6a -- Smith v Hughes, COUNTERFACTUAL: the risk not borne`

-- NOT A CASE. One field moved from fixture 6 -- `the mistaken party bears the
-- risk` set to FALSE -- to isolate what the term/assumption line is doing.
-- With risk out of the way the American rule reaches a known mistake as to
-- quality, and the other three still do not.

`Smith v Hughes -- counterfactual, risk not borne` MEANS `Mistake case` WITH
    `the case` IS "COUNTERFACTUAL: Smith v Hughes with the risk of the mistake not borne by the buyer"
    `what the mistaken party was mistaken about` IS `a basic assumption that is not a term`
    `the mistake was fundamental` IS FALSE
    `what the other party knew` IS `actual knowledge of the mistake`
    `what the other party did` IS `a conscious omission to disabuse`
    `the mistake had a material adverse effect on the exchange` IS TRUE
    `the mistaken party bears the risk of the mistake` IS FALSE
    `the other party's fault caused the mistake` IS FALSE
    `enforcement would be unconscionable` IS FALSE

#ASSERT `what the Restatement does` `Smith v Hughes -- counterfactual, risk not borne` EQUALS `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`
#ASSERT `what Singapore law does` `Smith v Hughes -- counterfactual, risk not borne` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what English law does` `Smith v Hughes -- counterfactual, risk not borne` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what Taylor v Johnson does` `Smith v Hughes -- counterfactual, risk not borne` EQUALS `the contract stands`

§§ `7 -- Donovan v RRL Corp`

-- A newspaper's proofreading error advertised a used Jaguar at US$25,995
-- when the dealer's price was US$37,995. The buyer "was unaware of the
-- mistake before it was disclosed to him". The Supreme Court of California
-- adopted Restatement s 153(a) as California law and allowed rescission.
--
-- THE SHARPEST DIVERGENCE IN THE CORPUS. Nobody knew anything and nobody did
-- anything improper, so all three Commonwealth tests stop at their first
-- condition. The American rule never asks about the other party's state of
-- mind on this limb at all.

`Donovan v RRL Corp` MEANS `Mistake case` WITH
    `the case` IS "Donovan v RRL Corp (2001) 26 Cal 4th 261"
    `what the mistaken party was mistaken about` IS `a term of the contract`
    `the mistake was fundamental` IS TRUE
    `what the other party knew` IS `neither knowledge nor reason to suspect`
    `what the other party did` IS `nothing beyond accepting the bargain`
    `the mistake had a material adverse effect on the exchange` IS TRUE
    `the mistaken party bears the risk of the mistake` IS FALSE
    `the other party's fault caused the mistake` IS FALSE
    `enforcement would be unconscionable` IS TRUE

-- United States: HELD. Rescission allowed.
#ASSERT `what the Restatement does` `Donovan v RRL Corp` EQUALS `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`
#ASSERT `enforcement would be unconscionable under section 153(a)` `Donovan v RRL Corp`
#ASSERT NOT (`the other party had reason to know or was at fault` `Donovan v RRL Corp`)
-- The three Commonwealth jurisdictions, computed, all decline.
#ASSERT `what Singapore law does` `Donovan v RRL Corp` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what English law does` `Donovan v RRL Corp` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what Taylor v Johnson does` `Donovan v RRL Corp` EQUALS `the contract stands`

§§ `8 -- Quoine Pte Ltd v B2C2 Ltd`

-- Algorithmic trades on a cryptocurrency exchange executed at roughly 250
-- times the market rate when the platform's own systems failed. Quoine Pte
-- Ltd v B2C2 Ltd [2020] SGCA(I) 02 at [16]: "The Counterparties' mistaken
-- belief that they were buying ETH for BTC at prices that did not deviate
-- significantly from the market price was not a mistake as to a term of the
-- Trading Contracts. Rather, this was a mistake as to the premise on which
-- the buy orders were placed, or a mistaken assumption that the Platform
-- would not fail. Even assuming there was an operative mistake, Mr Boonen did
-- not have actual or constructive knowledge of the Counterparties' mistaken
-- belief (at [114], [115] and [126])."
--
-- The doctrine failing twice over, on modern facts, and the case that
-- confirms Chwee Kin Keong is still the test fifteen years on.

`Quoine v B2C2` MEANS `Mistake case` WITH
    `the case` IS "Quoine Pte Ltd v B2C2 Ltd [2020] SGCA(I) 02"
    `what the mistaken party was mistaken about` IS `a basic assumption that is not a term`
    `the mistake was fundamental` IS FALSE
    `what the other party knew` IS `neither knowledge nor reason to suspect`
    `what the other party did` IS `nothing beyond accepting the bargain`
    `the mistake had a material adverse effect on the exchange` IS TRUE
    `the mistaken party bears the risk of the mistake` IS FALSE
    `the other party's fault caused the mistake` IS FALSE
    `enforcement would be unconscionable` IS FALSE

-- Singapore: HELD. Both limbs rejected.
#ASSERT `what Singapore law does` `Quoine v B2C2` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT NOT (`the mistake is of a kind the doctrine reaches` `Quoine v B2C2`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the other party at least had reason to suspect` `Quoine v B2C2`)
#ASSERT `what English law does` `Quoine v B2C2` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what Taylor v Johnson does` `Quoine v B2C2` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what the Restatement does` `Quoine v B2C2` EQUALS `the contract stands`

§§ `8a -- Quoine, COUNTERFACTUAL: enforcement characterised as unconscionable`

-- NOT A CASE, and no court has so characterised it. One field moved from
-- fixture 8. It exists because the Singapore doctrine cannot reach these
-- facts at all -- the mistake was not as to a term, so the inquiry stops
-- before unconscionability is ever asked -- while the American rule reaches
-- them on s 153(a) without asking about knowledge either. It is the cleanest
-- statement of what the term/assumption line costs.

`Quoine -- counterfactual, enforcement unconscionable` MEANS `Mistake case` WITH
    `the case` IS "COUNTERFACTUAL: Quoine v B2C2 with enforcement of the trades characterised as unconscionable"
    `what the mistaken party was mistaken about` IS `a basic assumption that is not a term`
    `the mistake was fundamental` IS FALSE
    `what the other party knew` IS `neither knowledge nor reason to suspect`
    `what the other party did` IS `nothing beyond accepting the bargain`
    `the mistake had a material adverse effect on the exchange` IS TRUE
    `the mistaken party bears the risk of the mistake` IS FALSE
    `the other party's fault caused the mistake` IS FALSE
    `enforcement would be unconscionable` IS TRUE

#ASSERT `what the Restatement does` `Quoine -- counterfactual, enforcement unconscionable` EQUALS `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`
#ASSERT `what Singapore law does` `Quoine -- counterfactual, enforcement unconscionable` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what English law does` `Quoine -- counterfactual, enforcement unconscionable` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what Taylor v Johnson does` `Quoine -- counterfactual, enforcement unconscionable` EQUALS `the contract stands`

§ `The deltas, stated as assertions`

-- Everything above is per-case. This section asserts the COMPARATIVE claims
-- the report makes, so that a claim in the prose cannot drift away from what
-- the modules actually compute.

§§ `Delta 1 -- Singapore relieves where Australia's stated test does not`

-- The second appellant in Digilandmall: constructive knowledge plus a
-- conscious omission. Chwee Kin Keong at [73] is explicit that this is where
-- it parts company with Taylor v Johnson.
#ASSERT (`what Singapore law does` `Digilandmall -- the second appellant`) `diverges from` (`what Taylor v Johnson does` `Digilandmall -- the second appellant`)
#ASSERT `the mistaken party is relieved` (`what Singapore law does` `Digilandmall -- the second appellant`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the mistaken party is relieved` (`what Taylor v Johnson does` `Digilandmall -- the second appellant`))

§§ `Delta 2 -- void against voidable, which only a third party feels`

-- On an actually-known fundamental mistake as to a term, Singapore voids and
-- the United States makes voidable. Both relieve the mistaken party. The
-- difference falls entirely on someone who was not in the room.
#ASSERT (`what Singapore law does` `Digilandmall -- the third appellant`) `differs from, only as to third parties,` (`what the Restatement does` `Digilandmall -- the third appellant`)
#ASSERT NOT (`a good faith purchaser for value without notice keeps what he bought` (`what Singapore law does` `Digilandmall -- the third appellant`))
#ASSERT `a good faith purchaser for value without notice keeps what he bought` (`what the Restatement does` `Digilandmall -- the third appellant`)

§§ `Delta 3 -- the United States relieves against an innocent counterparty`

-- Donovan. Nobody knew, nobody did anything improper, and only the American
-- rule gives relief. This is the widest gap in the corpus.
#ASSERT `the mistaken party is relieved` (`what the Restatement does` `Donovan v RRL Corp`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the mistaken party is relieved` (`what Singapore law does` `Donovan v RRL Corp`))
#ASSERT NOT (`the mistaken party is relieved` (`what Taylor v Johnson does` `Donovan v RRL Corp`))
#ASSERT (`what the Restatement does` `Donovan v RRL Corp`) `diverges from` (`what Singapore law does` `Donovan v RRL Corp`)

§§ `Delta 4 -- England cannot answer the question Singapore answered`

-- The class Chwee Kin Keong at [80] decided is precisely the class Great
-- Peace Shipping left open. Both Digilandmall's second appellant and Taylor
-- v Johnson fall in it.
#ASSERT REFUSED `what English law does` `Digilandmall -- the second appellant`
#ASSERT REFUSED `what English law does` `Taylor v Johnson`
-- And England answers everywhere else in the battery.
#ASSERT `what English law does` `Digilandmall -- the third appellant` EQUALS `the contract is void`
#ASSERT `what English law does` `Smith v Hughes` EQUALS `the contract stands`
#ASSERT `what English law does` `Donovan v RRL Corp` EQUALS `the contract stands`

§§ `Delta 5 -- the three tests agree on the control case`

-- Riverlate. No knowledge, no impropriety, no relief anywhere, and the
-- American rule stops too because the lessor bore its own solicitors' risk.
#ASSERT NOT (`the mistaken party is relieved` (`what Singapore law does` `Riverlate Properties v Paul`))
#ASSERT NOT (`the mistaken party is relieved` (`what English law does` `Riverlate Properties v Paul`))
#ASSERT NOT (`the mistaken party is relieved` (`what Taylor v Johnson does` `Riverlate Properties v Paul`))
#ASSERT NOT (`the mistaken party is relieved` (`what the Restatement does` `Riverlate Properties v Paul`))
